::ALG2_U04_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A city population of 50000 grows by 2% annually. Which model fits? {
= P(t)=50000(1.02)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Use the initial population and growth factor 1.02.
~ P(t)=50000(0.98)<sup>t</sup>#This models 2% decay.
~ P(t)=1.02(50000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the parameters.
~ P(t)=50000+2t#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A car worth 24000 depreciates 14% annually. Which model fits? {
~ V(t)=24000(1.14)<sup>t</sup>#This models appreciation.
= V(t)=24000(0.86)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Losing 14% means 86% remains.
~ V(t)=0.86(24000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
~ V(t)=24000-14t#This is fixed-dollar decrease.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A sample starts at 64 mg and halves every hour. Which model fits? {
~ A(t)=64(2)<sup>t</sup>#This doubles instead of halves.
~ A(t)=0.5(64)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
= A(t)=64(0.5)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Half-life means the amount is multiplied by 0.5 each hour.
~ A(t)=64-0.5t#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 1000(1.05)<sup>t</sup>=2000 for t. {
~ t = log(1.05)/log(2)#This reverses the ratio.
~ t = 2/1.05#This treats the equation as linear.
~ t = 1000#This subtracts values rather than solving for time.
= t = log(2)/log(1.05)#Correct. Divide by 1000 to get 1.05<sup>t</sup>=2, then use logarithms.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Solve 80(0.75)<sup>t</sup>=20 for t. {
= t = log(20/80)/log(0.75)#Correct. Divide by 80, then use logarithms.
~ t = log(0.75)/log(20/80)#This reverses the ratio.
~ t = 60/0.75#This treats the model as linear.
~ t = 20 - 80 - 0.75#This does not solve the exponent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does the 800 represent in M(t)=800(1.06)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ The growth rate#The growth rate is 6%.
= The initial amount#Correct. At t=0, the model output is 800.
~ The time#The variable t represents time.
~ The final amount#The final amount depends on t.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>What does the 1.06 represent in M(t)=800(1.06)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ 106% growth per time period#The amount becomes 106% of previous, which is 6% growth.
~ 6% decay per time period#A factor greater than 1 is growth.
= 6% growth per time period#Correct. The factor 1.06 means 100% plus 6%.
~ Initial amount of 1.06#The initial amount is 800.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A model has factor 0.73. What is the decay rate? {
~ 73%#That is the percent remaining.
~ 0.73%#This confuses factor and percent.
~ 127%#This does not describe decay.
= 27%#Correct. If 73% remains, 27% is lost.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A quantity doubles every 6 hours from 30. Which model uses t in 6-hour periods? {
= A(t)=30(2)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. If t counts 6-hour periods, the base is 2.
~ A(t)=2(30)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
~ A(t)=30(6)<sup>t</sup>#The 6 describes the time unit, not the factor.
~ A(t)=30+2t#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A quantity has half-life 4 days from 100. Which model uses t in 4-day periods? {
~ A(t)=100(2)<sup>t</sup>#This doubles instead of halves.
= A(t)=100(0.5)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Each 4-day period keeps half.
~ A(t)=0.5(100)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
~ A(t)=100-4t#This is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which answer best interprets t=12 in a growth model? {
~ The final amount is 12 dollars.#The equation solved for time, not amount.
~ The growth rate is 12%.#The rate is part of the factor, not the solved t.
= It takes about 12 time periods to reach the target.#Correct. The variable t represents time, so interpretation needs time units.
~ The initial amount is 12.#The initial amount is the coefficient.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which model best represents repeated 9% growth from 700? {
~ A(t)=700+9t#This is fixed amount growth.
~ A(t)=700(0.91)<sup>t</sup>#This models 9% decay.
~ A(t)=1.09(700)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
= A(t)=700(1.09)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Repeated percent growth is exponential.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which model best represents repeated 9% decay from 700? {
= A(t)=700(0.91)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Repeated percent decay uses factor 1 - 0.09 = 0.91.
~ A(t)=700(1.09)<sup>t</sup>#This models growth.
~ A(t)=700-9t#This subtracts a fixed amount.
~ A(t)=0.91(700)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>To find when 400(1.08)<sup>t</sup> reaches 1000, what is the first algebraic step? {
~ Take log of 400 only.#The whole equation must be handled consistently.
= Divide both sides by 400.#Correct. This isolates the exponential factor before using logarithms.
~ Subtract 1.08 from both sides.#The variable is in an exponent, not a linear term.
~ Divide both sides by t.#The variable is not a factor.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>To solve 30(0.6)<sup>t</sup>=5, why is the solution positive? {
~ The base is greater than 1.#The factor is 0.6, which is less than 1.
~ The initial amount is smaller than the target.#The initial amount is larger than the target.
= A factor below 1 needs positive time to decrease from 30 to 5.#Correct. Repeated multiplication by 0.6 decreases the amount over time.
~ Logarithms always give positive answers.#Logarithms can produce positive or negative values depending on the equation.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which situation needs a logarithm to solve directly? {
~ Finding A(3) for A(t)=1000(1.04)<sup>t</sup>.#This is direct substitution.
~ Finding the initial value in A(t)=1000(1.04)<sup>t</sup>.#The initial value is visible.
~ Finding the growth rate from factor 1.04.#This is parameter interpretation.
= Finding when an account reaches 3000 in A(t)=1000(1.04)<sup>t</sup>.#Correct. The unknown is in the exponent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A(t)=50(1.2)<sup>t</sup>. What is A(2)? {
= 72#Correct. Compute 50 times 1.2 squared.
~ 60#This is A(1).
~ 120#This doubles 60 incorrectly.
~ 52.4#This adds rather than multiplies.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A(t)=80(0.5)<sup>t</sup>. What is A(3)? {
~ 40#This is A(1).
= 10#Correct. Compute 80 times 0.5 cubed.
~ 20#This is A(2).
~ 5#This is A(4).
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is the best context interpretation for V(t)=18000(0.82)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ The value starts at 0.82 and grows to 18000.#This reverses parameters.
~ The value gains 82% each period.#A factor less than 1 is decay.
= The value starts at 18000 and loses 18% each time period.#Correct. A factor of 0.82 means 82% remains, so 18% is lost.
~ The value loses 82 dollars each period.#The change is percent-based, not a fixed dollar amount.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is the best context interpretation for P(t)=1200(1.15)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ The amount starts at 1.15 and grows by 1200.#This reverses parameters.
~ The amount decays 15% each time period.#A factor greater than 1 is growth.
~ The amount grows by 15 dollars each period.#The change is percent-based, not fixed dollars.
= The amount starts at 1200 and grows 15% each time period.#Correct. A factor of 1.15 means 15% growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>If a model output is measured in dollars and t is years, what units should a solved t use? {
= years#Correct. The variable t represents years in the model.
~ dollars#Dollars are output units, not time units.
~ percent#Percent describes the rate.
~ no units#Application answers should include units.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which formula solves a( b )<sup>t</sup>=c for t? {
~ t = log(b)/log(c/a)#This reverses the logarithm ratio.
= t = log(c/a)/log(b)#Correct. Divide by a, then use logarithms.
~ t = c/(ab)#This treats the model as linear.
~ t = c - a - b#This uses subtraction instead of inverse operations.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A value grows from 50 to 200 with factor 2 each period. How many periods? {
~ 1#One period reaches 100.
~ 3#Three periods reaches 400.
= 2#Correct. 50 doubles to 100 and doubles again to 200.
~ 4#Four periods reaches 800.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>A value decays from 160 to 20 with factor 0.5 each period. How many periods? {
~ 2#Two periods reaches 40.
~ 4#Four periods reaches 10.
~ 8#This is not the number of halvings.
= 3#Correct. 160 to 80 to 40 to 20 takes three half-life periods.
}

::ALG2_U04_L07_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L07_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.LOG.3<br><br>Question:<br>What should final application answers include? {
= A numerical result and contextual interpretation.#Correct. Applications require both calculation and meaning.
~ Only an equation.#An equation alone does not answer the question.
~ Only the rate.#The rate may not answer the target question.
~ Only the graph shape.#Applications usually require a context-specific result.
}

